  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x)]^{\u2032}}{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{3{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
